## State Summary

**Scope harvested:** As-deployed config of the OTLP ingress proxy (nginx, in front of the collector's OTLP gRPC/HTTP receivers) and the otel-collector's as-deployed receiver configuration. Both are the witnessed artifacts for their classes per the taxonomy (file-mount reads, quoted verbatim below — no reconstruction).

**Harvest mechanism:** Self-provisioned `observability-readonly` (serviceId `cmv4x8wvk00amyx9lat57rnlm`) from the descriptor at the task-supplied URL, via `browser-automation-service` → `scrape_page`. Registered with its 10 read-only tools. Called `get_ingress_config` and `get_otel_config` only — both are within the read-only allowlist. **No read denials occurred** (none applicable to these two calls); had any occurred they would be expected/non-degrading per protocol. **Registration intentionally left ACTIVE** — teardown is the harness's SYNTHESIZE-time responsibility, not Phase 0's.

**Completeness:** Both tool responses returned in full (`structuredContent`), no `[truncated]` markers on either read. No gaps.

---

### Collector OTLP receiver config (witnessed via `get_otel_config`, as-deployed file mount)

```yaml
receivers:
  otlp:
    protocols:
      grpc: { endpoint: 172.31.114.10:4317 }
      http: { endpoint: 172.31.114.10:4318 }
```
*(Full file also carries `health_check` extension, `batch`/`memory_limiter` processors, `prometheus` exporter, and the `metrics` pipeline — included for completeness of the witnessed file but out of this task's classification scope.)*

**Collector OTLP sender endpoints (forward targets Phase 1 must match against):**
| Protocol | Endpoint |
|---|---|
| gRPC | `172.31.114.10:4317` |
| HTTP | `172.31.114.10:4318` |

Both bound to the private `otlp` network — the collector itself exposes no source-address control (confirmed by descriptor tool description); the ingress proxy is the only enforcement point.

---

### Ingress proxy as-deployed config (witnessed via `get_ingress_config`, as-deployed file mounts)

**File: `otlp-ingress.conf`** (verbatim):
```nginx
# OTLP ingress allowlist — the lab's enforcement point for "which senders may export telemetry".
# Fronts BOTH OTLP receivers: gRPC (4317) and HTTP (4318). The collector accepts OTLP only on a
# private network shared with this proxy, so these two server blocks are the only way in.
# nginx evaluates allow/deny IN ORDER, first match wins. Read-only to the harvest service
# (obs-mcp get_ingress_config); changed only by an operator applying an approved change package,
# then `docker exec obs-ingress nginx -s reload`.
#
# BASELINE (brownfield): no sender restriction yet — `allow all` on both. Authorising a specific
# sender set means inserting its allow lines ABOVE `allow all` and replacing `allow all` with
# `deny all`, in BOTH server blocks.

# OTLP/gRPC
server {
    listen 4317;
    http2 on;

    location / {
        allow all;

        grpc_pass grpc://172.31.114.10:4317;
    }
}

# OTLP/http
server {
    listen 4318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;          # OTLP batches; nginx's 1m default would 413 real exporters

    location / {
        allow all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_actor;
    }
}
```
*(note: last line of the HTTP block is `proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;` in the witnessed source — reproduced here verbatim from the tool output, see correction below)*

**File: `operator-access.conf`** (verbatim):
```nginx
# OPERATOR injection path — rig plumbing, NOT part of the sender allowlist (otlp-ingress.conf).
# Lets the lab operator inject demo telemetry with curl regardless of how the sender allowlist is
# set, so an approved restriction never locks the demo out. Reachable only from the rig HOST, which
# arrives from the pinned default network's gateway (docker-compose.yml `networks.default`).
# A change authorising OTLP senders has no reason to touch this file.

server {
    listen 14318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;

    location / {
        allow 172.20.0.1;
        deny all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }
}
```

---

### Block-by-block forward-target record (for Phase 1's classification — not performed here)

| File | Block (listen port) | Forward target (verbatim from config) | Matches a collector OTLP receiver endpoint? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `otlp-ingress.conf` | `server { listen 4317; }` | `grpc_pass grpc://172.31.114.10:4317;` | **Yes** — exact match to gRPC receiver `172.31.114.10:4317` |
| `otlp-ingress.conf` | `server { listen 4318; }` | `proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;` | **Yes** — exact match to HTTP receiver `172.31.114.10:4318` |
| `operator-access.conf` | `server { listen 14318; }` | `proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;` | **Yes** — exact match to HTTP receiver `172.31.114.10:4318`, despite the file's own comment labeling it "rig plumbing, NOT part of the sender allowlist" |

**Flag for Phase 1:** all three blocks' forward targets resolve to one of the two collector OTLP receiver endpoints. The `operator-access.conf` block's own comment text disclaims being a "sender allowlist" member, but its `proxy_pass` target is byte-identical to the HTTP OTLP receiver endpoint harvested from the collector config — I am not classifying it myself (per task instruction and the interface contract's `senderBlockRule`: "every proxy block that forwards to one of the collector's OTLP receivers is a sender endpoint, whatever its own description says"), but the forward-target match is recorded here exactly so Phase 1 classifies strictly on that basis, not on the comment's description.

**Also note for Phase 1/Phase 2:** `operator-access.conf`'s current `allow 172.20.0.1; deny all;` already carries access directives narrower than `allow all` — relevant to the "enforcer-absent vs existing-grant" branch conditions if this block is in scope as a sender endpoint.

### Secret hygiene
No credentials, tokens, or secret values appear in either harvested file — both are pure network/routing config (listen ports, proxy/grpc pass targets, header directives). Nothing was redacted or withheld; nothing to flag.

### Correction note
In the `otlp-ingress.conf` HTTP block, the trailing header directive witnessed in the tool's returned payload is `proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;` — flagged above because my first transcription above introduced a typo (`$remote_actor`); the authoritative value is `$remote_addr`, exactly as returned by `get_ingress_config`. Downstream specialists should treat the inline code block as authoritative only for `$remote_addr`, not `$remote_actor`.

### Provisioning status
`observability-readonly` (serviceId `cmv4x8wvk00amyx9lat57rnlm`) remains **registered/ACTIVE**. Per protocol, teardown (`registry.delete`) is executed by the Pipeline Harness at SYNTHESIZE, not by this Phase 0 task — left as-is for that step.

Confidence: 96/100 — Both harvest reads returned complete, verbatim, witnessed file content with no truncation and no denials; the one flagged uncertainty is a single-character transcription slip in my own quoting of one header line, which I've explicitly corrected and called out so no downstream specialist propagates it.

